  "mixed-language": "# Study habits across languages\n\nGood study habits are easier to maintain when a learner connects new ideas\nwith familiar language. Spaced practice means reviewing material at increasing\nintervals instead of cramming everything into one evening. Retrieval practice\nmeans closing the book and trying to recall an idea, process, or example.\nShort explanations in the learner’s own words reveal gaps that rereading can\nhide.\n\nSome learners use more than one language while studying. Telugu: “చదువు\nనెమ్మదిగా, కానీ ప్రతిరోజూ.” Hindi: “अभ्यास से समझ गहरी होती है।” These lines\ncan be understood as encouragement to study steadily and build understanding\nthrough practice. The important point is not perfect translation; it is making\nthe idea memorable and checking its meaning.\n\nA useful routine has four parts. First, preview headings and write two\nquestions. Second, read one section and mark a few key terms. Third, close the\nsource and explain the section aloud or in notes. Fourth, compare the\nexplanation with the source and correct unsupported claims. A learner might\nschedule a five-minute review after one day, another after three days, and a\nthird after one week.\n\nLanguage details matter when notes are copied between systems. Accented words\nsuch as café, jalapeño, and über should remain intact. Smart punctuation and\nsymbols can carry meaning too. A dash—like this one—may separate an aside,\nwhile a mathematical symbol can make a definition concise. Unicode is not a\ndecoration; it is part of the learner’s document and should survive storage,\nanalysis, export, and display.\n\nThe best routine is one the learner can repeat. Set a small target, record\nwhat was recalled, and ask a precise question when something remains unclear.\n",
  "science-photosynthesis": "# Photosynthesis and the carbon cycle\n\nPhotosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria capture\nlight energy and store it as chemical energy. In green leaves, chlorophyll\nabsorbs mostly red and blue light. Water, H₂O, enters through the roots and\ntravels through vessels to the leaves. Carbon dioxide, CO₂, enters through\nstomata. In simplified form, light energy helps combine CO₂ and H₂O to make\nglucose and oxygen, O₂. The balanced equation is often written as\n6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, although the real pathway contains many steps.\n\nThe light-dependent reactions occur in thylakoid membranes. They split water,\nrelease O₂, and produce energy carriers. The Calvin cycle occurs in the\nstroma. It uses carbon dioxide and those energy carriers to build sugars.\nTemperature affects enzyme activity: a plant may photosynthesize more quickly\nas temperature rises, until heat damages proteins or increases water loss.\nMany plants have an optimum near a moderate range, not an unlimited response.\n\nThe carbon cycle moves carbon among the atmosphere, living organisms, soil,\noceans, and rocks. Plants remove atmospheric CO₂ during photosynthesis.\nRespiration returns CO₂ when organisms use stored food. Decomposition releases\ncarbon from dead material, while combustion transfers carbon from fuels to the\natmosphere. Oceans absorb and release CO₂, and some carbon becomes sediment.\n\nHuman activity changes the balance by burning coal, oil, and gas and by clearing\nforests. More atmospheric CO₂ can increase photosynthesis for some plants, but\nit also contributes to warming and ocean acidification. A useful approximation\nis that growth depends on several limits together: light, water, nutrients,\ntemperature, and CO₂. A graph may show x²-shaped growth only in a mathematical\nmodel, not a universal biological law. Scientists compare measurements and\nuncertainty; “≈” does not mean a number is exact. Smart quotes, an em—dash,\n°C, and µm can appear in notes without changing the underlying science.\n",
  "short-edge": "# Short edge sample\n\nSolar panels convert light into electricity. Batteries store energy for later use.\n",
